{"module":"place","voice":"male","duration":191.6,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Place: who gets to live where","start":0.0,"end":1.94,"words":[[0.05,0.47],[0.71,0.85],[0.85,1.13],[1.13,1.27],[1.27,1.55],[1.55,1.76]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Most American neighborhoods didn't become segregated by accident or by choice alone.","start":2.74,"end":8.1,"words":[[2.79,3.14],[3.14,3.69],[3.69,4.32],[4.32,4.66],[4.66,5.01],[5.01,5.77],[5.77,5.98],[5.98,6.54],[6.54,6.61],[6.61,6.82],[6.82,7.16],[7.16,7.58]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Governments drew the lines, and many of those lines are still visible on the map.","start":8.45,"end":14.22,"words":[[8.5,9.33],[9.33,9.58],[9.58,9.75],[9.75,10.25],[10.54,10.79],[10.79,11.12],[11.12,11.29],[11.29,11.62],[11.62,12.04],[12.04,12.12],[12.12,12.46],[12.46,13.04],[13.04,13.2],[13.2,13.37],[13.37,13.7]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Racial zoning, then covenants","start":14.57,"end":17.5,"words":[[14.61,15.18],[15.18,15.85],[16.18,16.46],[16.46,17.32]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Baltimore passed the first citywide racial zoning law in 1910; the Supreme Court struck racial zoning down in Buchanan v. Warley (1917).","start":18.2,"end":28.66,"words":[[18.25,18.8],[18.8,19.11],[19.11,19.27],[19.27,19.58],[19.58,20.21],[20.21,20.68],[20.68,21.15],[21.15,21.31],[21.31,21.46],[21.46,22.33],[22.6,22.76],[22.76,23.23],[23.23,23.46],[23.46,23.86],[23.86,24.33],[24.33,24.8],[24.8,25.11],[25.11,25.27],[25.27,25.9],[25.9,26.13],[26.52,26.84],[26.84,28.09]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Developers and neighbors turned to racial covenants written into deeds, which the Court let stand in 1926.","start":29.01,"end":37.24,"words":[[29.07,29.8],[29.8,30.05],[30.05,30.54],[30.54,30.86],[30.86,31.03],[31.03,31.52],[31.52,32.25],[32.25,32.66],[32.66,32.98],[32.98,33.39],[33.68,34.0],[34.0,34.17],[34.17,34.41],[34.41,34.65],[34.65,35.06],[35.06,35.23],[35.23,36.69]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In Shelley v. Kraemer (1948) it ruled courts couldn't enforce them, but the language remains in millions of deeds.","start":37.59,"end":46.19,"words":[[37.64,37.8],[37.8,38.11],[38.11,38.34],[38.74,39.13],[39.13,40.3],[40.57,40.73],[40.73,41.04],[41.04,41.36],[41.36,41.83],[41.83,42.22],[42.22,42.53],[42.8,43.04],[43.04,43.2],[43.2,43.82],[43.82,44.37],[44.37,44.53],[44.53,45.07],[45.07,45.23],[45.23,45.62]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Some states, such as Minnesota (2019), now let owners formally strike it.","start":46.54,"end":52.29,"words":[[46.58,46.8],[46.8,47.31],[47.56,47.85],[47.85,47.99],[47.99,48.64],[48.64,49.66],[49.91,50.13],[50.13,50.34],[50.34,50.7],[50.7,51.14],[51.14,51.57],[51.57,51.79]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The map's covenants layer shows more than 100,000 of them.","start":52.64,"end":57.38,"words":[[52.69,52.85],[52.85,53.17],[53.17,53.89],[53.89,54.21],[54.21,54.53],[54.53,54.69],[54.69,54.93],[54.93,56.28],[56.28,56.44],[56.44,56.76]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Redlining","start":58.28,"end":59.38,"words":[[58.33,59.22]]},{"el":"s1","text":"From 1935 to 1940 the federal Home Owners' Loan Corporation graded neighborhoods in about 200 cities; Black and immigrant neighborhoods were colored red as “hazardous.”","start":60.08,"end":71.04,"words":[[60.1,60.38],[60.38,61.43],[61.43,61.57],[61.57,62.34],[62.34,62.48],[62.48,62.97],[62.97,63.24],[63.24,63.59],[63.59,63.87],[63.87,64.57],[64.57,65.06],[65.06,65.69],[65.69,65.83],[65.83,66.18],[66.18,66.81],[66.81,67.23],[67.47,67.75],[67.75,67.96],[67.96,68.52],[68.52,69.15],[69.15,69.29],[69.29,69.64],[69.64,69.85],[69.85,69.99],[69.99,70.55]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Federal Housing Administration refused to insure loans in or near Black neighborhoods and recommended covenants.","start":71.39,"end":78.88,"words":[[71.44,71.59],[71.59,72.13],[72.13,72.58],[72.58,73.65],[73.65,74.18],[74.18,74.33],[74.33,74.71],[74.71,75.09],[75.09,75.25],[75.25,75.32],[75.32,75.55],[75.55,75.86],[75.86,76.54],[76.54,76.77],[76.77,77.53],[77.53,78.29]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Detroit in 1941, a developer built a half-mile wall to separate a new white subdivision from a Black neighborhood so the FHA would back its loans.","start":79.23,"end":90.37,"words":[[79.27,79.43],[79.43,80.0],[80.0,80.17],[80.17,81.39],[81.68,81.84],[81.84,82.49],[82.49,82.82],[82.82,82.98],[82.98,83.55],[83.55,83.8],[83.8,83.96],[83.96,84.45],[84.45,84.61],[84.61,84.86],[84.86,85.19],[85.19,86.0],[86.0,86.33],[86.33,86.49],[86.49,86.82],[86.82,87.47],[87.47,87.72],[87.72,87.88],[87.88,88.53],[88.53,88.78],[88.78,89.02],[89.02,89.27],[89.27,89.76]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Studies find formerly redlined areas today are poorer, have lower life expectancy, and run several degrees hotter in summer.","start":90.72,"end":99.93,"words":[[90.76,91.27],[91.27,91.69],[91.69,92.19],[92.19,92.86],[92.86,93.37],[93.37,93.79],[93.79,93.87],[93.87,94.38],[94.67,94.92],[94.92,95.26],[95.26,95.59],[95.59,96.6],[96.9,97.15],[97.15,97.4],[97.4,97.91],[97.91,98.41],[98.41,98.75],[98.75,98.91],[98.91,99.33]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Sundown towns and white flight","start":100.83,"end":103.14,"words":[[100.88,101.48],[101.48,101.91],[101.91,102.17],[102.17,102.51],[102.51,102.94]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Historian James Loewen documented hundreds of towns that kept Black residents out after dark, by sign, ordinance or violence (see the old laws tracker).","start":103.84,"end":114.72,"words":[[103.88,104.62],[104.62,105.11],[105.11,105.6],[105.6,106.49],[106.49,107.15],[107.15,107.31],[107.31,107.72],[107.72,107.96],[107.96,108.29],[108.29,108.62],[108.62,109.35],[109.35,109.6],[109.6,109.92],[109.92,110.25],[110.54,110.78],[110.78,111.19],[111.47,112.05],[112.05,112.13],[112.13,112.78],[112.78,112.94],[112.94,113.11],[113.11,113.43],[113.43,113.68],[113.68,114.17]]},{"el":"s2","text":"As Black families moved north and into new neighborhoods, real estate agents practiced “blockbusting,” scaring white owners into selling cheaply and reselling to Black families at a markup.","start":115.07,"end":126.61,"words":[[115.12,115.27],[115.27,115.57],[115.57,116.1],[116.1,116.41],[116.41,116.63],[116.63,116.86],[116.86,117.16],[117.16,117.39],[117.39,118.14],[118.41,118.71],[118.71,119.17],[119.17,119.77],[119.77,120.37],[120.37,121.21],[121.21,121.74],[121.74,122.04],[122.04,122.42],[122.42,122.72],[122.72,123.1],[123.1,123.55],[123.55,123.78],[123.78,124.31],[124.31,124.46],[124.46,124.76],[124.76,125.29],[125.29,125.44],[125.44,125.59],[125.59,126.05]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Fair Housing Act passed on April 11, 1968, a week after Martin Luther King Jr. was killed.","start":126.96,"end":135.08,"words":[[127.01,127.16],[127.16,127.39],[127.39,127.85],[127.85,128.08],[128.08,128.38],[128.38,128.54],[128.54,128.99],[128.99,129.53],[129.8,131.17],[131.44,131.59],[131.59,131.82],[131.82,132.12],[132.12,132.51],[132.51,132.81],[132.81,133.04],[133.04,133.5],[133.88,134.11],[134.11,134.49]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Does zoning still keep neighborhoods apart?","start":135.98,"end":138.54,"words":[[136.03,136.23],[136.23,136.65],[136.65,136.92],[136.92,137.13],[137.13,137.75],[137.75,138.1]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Explicit racial rules are illegal, but zoning that allows only single-family homes on large lots raises the price of entry to many neighborhoods.","start":139.24,"end":149.05,"words":[[139.29,140.01],[140.01,140.48],[140.48,140.8],[140.8,140.88],[140.88,141.44],[141.72,141.95],[141.95,142.43],[142.43,142.67],[142.67,143.07],[143.07,143.46],[143.46,144.42],[144.42,144.82],[144.82,144.97],[144.97,145.29],[145.29,145.61],[145.61,146.09],[146.09,146.25],[146.25,146.64],[146.64,146.8],[146.8,147.2],[147.2,147.36],[147.36,147.68],[147.68,148.47]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Researchers broadly agree such rules preserve economic segregation; they debate how much they preserve racial segregation today, compared with income differences, preferences and discrimination by landlords and lenders, which fair-housing tests still find.","start":149.4,"end":165.21,"words":[[149.45,150.02],[150.02,150.44],[150.44,150.73],[150.73,151.01],[151.01,151.29],[151.29,151.72],[151.72,152.28],[152.28,153.13],[153.38,153.59],[153.59,154.01],[154.01,154.23],[154.23,154.51],[154.51,154.72],[154.72,155.15],[155.15,155.57],[155.57,156.35],[156.35,156.77],[157.02,157.52],[157.52,157.73],[157.73,158.08],[158.08,158.79],[159.04,159.75],[159.75,159.96],[159.96,160.95],[160.95,161.16],[161.16,161.73],[161.73,161.94],[161.94,162.43],[162.68,162.96],[162.96,163.6],[163.6,163.96],[163.96,164.24],[164.24,164.66]]},{"el":"h4","text":"What to do","start":166.11,"end":167.09,"words":[[166.16,166.46],[166.46,166.65],[166.65,166.84]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Look up your neighborhood on the redlining and covenant layers.","start":167.79,"end":171.67,"words":[[167.83,168.05],[168.05,168.19],[168.19,168.34],[168.34,168.92],[168.92,169.07],[169.07,169.21],[169.21,169.87],[169.87,170.09],[170.09,170.67],[170.67,171.11]]},{"el":"s4","text":"If your deed has a covenant and your state allows it, have it struck.","start":172.02,"end":176.56,"words":[[172.07,172.22],[172.22,172.37],[172.37,172.6],[172.6,172.83],[172.83,172.99],[172.99,173.6],[173.6,173.83],[173.83,173.98],[173.98,174.37],[174.37,174.75],[174.75,174.98],[175.25,175.48],[175.48,175.63],[175.63,176.09]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Support fair-housing testing, inclusive zoning reform and down-payment help for first-time buyers in formerly redlined areas.","start":176.91,"end":186.23,"words":[[176.96,177.39],[177.39,178.18],[178.18,178.79],[179.09,179.79],[179.79,180.31],[180.31,180.75],[180.75,181.01],[181.01,181.97],[181.97,182.32],[182.32,182.49],[182.49,183.19],[183.19,183.63],[183.63,183.8],[183.8,184.32],[184.32,185.02],[185.02,185.63]]},{"el":"s4","text":"(See also the modules on wealth and on communities destroyed.)","start":186.58,"end":190.65,"words":[[186.61,186.76],[186.76,187.15],[187.15,187.31],[187.31,187.85],[187.85,188.0],[188.0,188.31],[188.31,188.54],[188.54,188.7],[188.7,189.47],[189.47,190.17]]}]}